---
import '../../styles/global.css';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import hero from '../../assets/hero.png';
const title = 'OSFUN — Exploring operating systems for the AI era.';
const description = 'Operating systems research and open-source exploration for the AI era. Starting with Linux, we explore how agents and computers can work together.';
---
<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><title>{title}</title><meta name="description" content={description}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="zh-CN" href="https://osfun.org/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://osfun.org/en/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://osfun.org/"/><link rel="canonical" href="https://osfun.org/en/"/><meta property="og:title" content={title}/><meta property="og:description" content={description}/><meta property="og:type" content="website"/><meta property="og:url" content="https://osfun.org/en/"/><meta property="og:image" content="https://osfun.org/social.png"/><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"/></head><body class="lab english"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><div class="wrap"><header><a class="brand" href="/en/">OSFUN<small>OPEN SYSTEMS RESEARCH</small></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="#explore">Research</a><a href="#about">Approach</a><a href="#join">Get involved ↗</a><a class="language-switch" href="/" lang="zh-CN" hreflang="zh-CN" aria-label="切换到中文">中文 ↗</a></nav></header><main id="main"><section class="hero"><div><h1>The next operating system<br/>starts with curiosity.</h1><p>Operating systems research and open-source exploration for the AI era. Starting with Linux, we explore how agents and computers can work together.</p><a class="button" href="#explore">Explore the research ↗</a><p class="small">OPEN QUESTIONS. REPRODUCIBLE EXPLORATIONS.</p></div><figure><Image src={hero} widths={[480,800,1200]} sizes="(max-width:760px) 100vw, 50vw" format="webp" loading="eager" alt="Concept illustration of three transparent computing layers connected by green light"/><figcaption>RUNTIME / TRUST / FOUNDATION · AI-generated concept image</figcaption></figure></section><section class="section" id="explore"><div class="section-title"><h2>Research</h2><p>Rethinking system boundaries for the AI era.</p></div><div class="grid"><article class="entry"><span class="index">01 / RUNTIME</span><h3>Agent runtimes</h3><p>How should a system manage the lifecycle, state and resources of agents running ongoing tasks?</p><details><summary>Questions to start with</summary><div><p>How do interrupted tasks recover? How should agents share CPU, memory and tools? How can execution be observable and reproducible?</p></div></details></article><article class="entry"><span class="index">02 / TRUST</span><h3>Permissions & trust</h3><p>Ground autonomous execution in permissions that can be understood, revoked and audited.</p><details><summary>Questions to start with</summary><div><p>What are the minimum permissions for a task? How do we isolate untrusted code, record tool calls and let people revoke access at any time?</p></div></details></article><article class="entry"><span class="index">03 / FOUNDATION</span><h3>Linux & foundations</h3><p>Build on existing open-source systems to explore kernels, isolation, scheduling and interaction.</p><details><summary>Questions to start with</summary><div><p>What belongs in the kernel and what belongs in user space? How can containers and virtual machines support agents? How do we build reproducible Linux experiments?</p></div></details></article></div><p class="note">These research topics are evolving. Expand a direction to explore its starting questions.</p></section><section class="section" id="about"><div class="statement"><h2>Start with questions.<br/>Let experiments speak.</h2><p>OSFUN is an early-stage research interest site. We explore systems questions for the AI era while building on decades of operating systems work. We start with testable questions and document experimental conditions, processes and limitations. These are directions for exploration, not claims of a finished system or published findings.</p></div><ol class="steps"><li><span class="index">01</span><strong>Frame a question</strong><p>Define the scenario and hypothesis. Study relevant system designs and open-source implementations.</p></li><li><span class="index">02</span><strong>Reproduce an experiment</strong><p>Record the environment, steps and observations. Distinguish facts, assumptions and conclusions.</p></li><li><span class="index">03</span><strong>Discuss openly</strong><p>Share notes and reproducible materials, then iterate based on feedback.</p></li></ol><p class="note">Start with open-source foundations: <a href="https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Linux kernel documentation ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">QEMU documentation ↗</a></p></section><section class="section contact" id="join"><div><h2>Bring a question. Explore together.</h2><p>Join the conversation on system design, Linux experiments, agent runtimes and open-source practice. Bring a question, a research note or a reproducible experiment.</p></div><a class="button" href="mailto:info@airdb.com?subject=OSFUN%3A%20Research%20discussion">Discuss an idea ↗</a></section></main><footer><span>© {new Date().getFullYear()} OSFUN · Stay curious. Explore openly.</span><div><a href="https://airdb.team/">Meet the makers ↗</a><a href="https://wefun.org/">WEFUN ↗</a><a href="#main">Back to top ↑</a></div></footer></div></body></html>